{{afc comment|Wikidata entry [[d:Q11644858]] Wikidata name: Important Tangible Folk Cultural Property [[User:Immanuelle|Immanuelle]] ([[User talk:Immanuelle|talk]]) 08:29, 11 June 2025 (UTC)}} {{Unreferenced|date=June 2017}} '''Important Tangible Folk Cultural Properties''' (重要有形民俗文化財, Jūyō Yūkei Minzoku Bunkazai) are items designated by the Japanese government under the [[Cultural Properties Protection Act|Cultural Properties Protection Law of Japan]] as particularly significant examples of tangible folk cultural properties. These include items related to customs, faith, and life such as [[:en:Clothing|Clothing]], [[:en:tools|tools]], and [[:en:housing|housing]] used in the practice of [[:en:folk customs|folk customs]], [[folk performing arts]], or folk techniques.
== Position under the Cultural Properties Protection Act ==
The Cultural Properties Protection Act, established in [[:en:1950|1950]] (Showa 25), initially categorized these as [[folkloric materials]] under [[:en:Tangible Cultural Property (Japan)|Tangible Cultural Property (Japan)]]. The 1954 (Showa 29) amendments made folkloric materials independent of tangible cultural properties and defined their concept in law. Items of particular importance were designated as "Important Folkloric Materials." In the 1975 (Showa 50) amendments, this designation was renamed to Important Tangible Folk Cultural Properties.
As of March 21, 2024 (Reiwa 6), 227 items are designated.
